1
00:00:05,878 --> 00:00:06,805
Hello, judges.

2
00:00:07,190 --> 00:00:09,525
Thank you for inviting us to Round 2.

3
00:00:10,255 --> 00:00:12,625
We are happy to present our demo.

4
00:00:13,695 --> 00:00:18,495
Our company operates in Denmark, Norway, and Sweden.

5
00:00:18,535 --> 00:00:21,885
After a merger, teams were
left with disconnected systems.

6
00:00:22,275 --> 00:00:29,385
Staff had to enter orders again, customers had
to wait, and stock information could change.

7
00:00:29,834 --> 00:00:36,915
These are our three main problems: repeated
entry, waiting customers, and changing stock.

8
00:00:37,795 --> 00:00:47,645
Round 2 asks another question: can the system
respond reliably when AI or the POS has an error?

9
00:00:48,385 --> 00:00:52,455
SiteDesk works alongside the POS and ERP.

10
00:00:52,985 --> 00:00:57,495
We will show the first user journey at the counter,

11
00:00:58,105 --> 00:01:03,015
the second journey by email,
and then AI and POS failures.

12
00:01:10,253 --> 00:01:17,728
First, the counter journey. I will
upload the customer's 18-item list.

13
00:01:18,458 --> 00:01:27,078
SiteDesk reads the list, matches products,
converts quantities, and checks stock.

14
00:01:27,568 --> 00:01:31,768
Items that need attention appear for staff to review.

15
00:01:32,358 --> 00:01:38,648
We can offer an alternative, but we only
choose it after the customer agrees.

16
00:01:39,718 --> 00:01:41,868
Now I will confirm the order.

17
00:01:42,148 --> 00:01:48,448
The POS accepts it and returns a reference.

18
00:01:53,246 --> 00:01:56,741
Now the customer is not at the counter.

19
00:01:56,781 --> 00:02:10,421
We paste the customer's email into the quote flow.

20
00:02:10,461 --> 00:02:17,531
SiteDesk finds the unavailable items
and prepares a reply with alternatives.

21
00:02:17,681 --> 00:02:23,801
The staff sends the reply, and the customer approves it.

22
00:02:23,841 --> 00:02:25,301
We save the quote.

23
00:02:25,341 --> 00:02:35,291
It shows a price and stock snapshot,
but it does not reserve the items.

24
00:02:36,221 --> 00:02:42,561
Before pickup, I change the stock in
the demo and check the quote again.

25
00:02:43,381 --> 00:02:51,741
Staff can review the changes, prepare the pick slip,
and confirm the order when the customer arrives.

26
00:02:56,768 --> 00:03:02,244
Now we will show what happens
when the POS is unavailable.

27
00:03:02,284 --> 00:03:06,254
SiteDesk exports the order to a CSV file.

28
00:03:06,294 --> 00:03:16,784
The status clearly says that the POS did not accept it.

29
00:03:16,824 --> 00:03:25,264
Staff can download the file for manual entry.

30
00:03:29,324 --> 00:03:33,744
Next, the AI is unavailable.

31
00:03:34,724 --> 00:03:40,534
SiteDesk shows an alert and lets
staff search the catalog manually.

32
00:03:41,574 --> 00:03:48,414
Staff can select the correct item
and continue the order to the POS.

33
00:03:49,064 --> 00:03:51,354
The technical checks still apply.

34
00:03:57,597 --> 00:04:00,387
Here is our system architecture.

35
00:04:00,867 --> 00:04:08,367
The web app connects to the FastAPI core, which handles product matching and orders.

36
00:04:08,887 --> 00:04:14,977
It uses local embeddings, hard rules, and PostgreSQL.

37
00:04:15,437 --> 00:04:22,417
The POS connector is part of the core and connects to our simulated POS.

38
00:04:23,227 --> 00:04:27,097
If the POS fails, SiteDesk exports a CSV.

39
00:04:27,427 --> 00:04:29,167
The LLM is optional.

40
00:04:35,832 --> 00:04:39,312
We showed the counter journey, the email quote journey,

41
00:04:39,512 --> 00:04:45,682
a stock change, and recovery from AI and POS failures.

42
00:04:46,212 --> 00:04:54,622
These cases address our three main problems: repeated
entry, waiting customers, and changing stock.

43
00:04:55,482 --> 00:04:59,942
They also show the reliability work for Round 2.

44
00:05:00,732 --> 00:05:03,432
This is a demo with simulated systems.

45
00:05:04,122 --> 00:05:08,682
More testing is needed with real store data and systems.

46
00:05:13,545 --> 00:05:18,415
This table summarizes what we showed and what still needs testing.

47
00:05:19,065 --> 00:05:26,835
The demo addresses repeated order entry, customer waiting, and changing stock through both order journeys.

48
00:05:27,635 --> 00:05:33,005
We also showed how the system responds to AI and POS failures.

49
00:05:33,455 --> 00:05:39,335
Real-store performance and production integrations still need testing.

